{
  "schema": "ld-s10y-image/figure-spec@2",
  "id": "fig-143",
  "mode": "deterministic",
  "description": "Seven incident levels in one frame: six off-axis solid parallel rays toward a concave mirror and the shared central axis. Six reflected paths cross the model F and continue left, with reversed arrow directions. The common crossing is explicitly the paraxial approximation, schematic and not to scale; not exact arbitrary-aperture spherical focusing.",
  "source": {
    "image": {
      "path": "ssot-resources/soviet10year-textbooks/artifacts/10p/figures/fig-143.png",
      "sha256": "4e6d4f7232fb0df2573d25fea439cd18f6a90bae01837603dd37840a98550aa2"
    },
    "authoritativeText": [
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s2-n70",
        "text": "Figure 142"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s2-n70",
        "text": "For a concave mirror, paraxial rays parallel to the principal axis approximately converge after reflection at the **principal focus**; see Figure 143 and footnote ①.\nModern clarification: This principal focus of a concave mirror is a **real focus**, because the actual reflected rays converge in front of the mirror, rather than only their extensions intersecting."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s2-n70",
        "text": "① The source requires the distance of the rays from the principal axis to be small compared with the radius of the mirror's sphere before treating the reflected rays as approximately meeting at one point.\nModern clarification: Parallel rays farther from the axis do not meet at exactly the same point as paraxial rays; this departure is called **spherical aberration**.\nA spherical mirror is therefore not an exact point focuser for beams of arbitrary width."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s2-n70",
        "text": "Figure 143"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s2-n70",
        "text": "If a parallel beam approaches a concave mirror along a secondary axis, its approximate focus is displaced from the principal focus under the small-angle, narrow-beam approximation; Figure 144 is associated with this case.\nThe source treats the distances of these points from the mirror as equal to the distance of the principal focus, a statement to be understood within the same approximate model.\nTaken together, the approximate focusing positions for beams arriving from different directions form a focal surface.\n\nWhen the angles between the secondary axes being considered and the principal axis are small, the focal surface near the principal focus can be approximated by a plane perpendicular to the principal axis, called the **focal plane**.\nModern clarification: This is not an exact equal-distance plane for beams at arbitrary large angles to the axis; large-angle beams can also have off-axis aberrations.\nThe focal-plane approximation does not remove the focusing limitations of a spherical mirror."
      }
    ],
